      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2930.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 695,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental prerequisite for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (Why Water is Weird)\nIn physics and chemistry, water is actually a very unusual substance. Most liquids shrink when they freeze, but water **expands**. Most substances are densest in their solid form, but water is densest as a liquid at around 4°C (39°F). This is why ice floats. \n\nWater also has a very high **heat capacity**, meaning it can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This property allows the oceans to regulate the temperature of the entire planet.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and use for agriculture (found in rivers, lakes, and oceans).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and polar caps.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and fog.\n\n### 3. \"The Universal Solvent\"\nWater is often called the universal solvent because it dissolves more substances than any other liquid. Because of the way its molecules are shaped (with a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side), it easily attracts and breaks apart other molecules, like salt and sugar. This is how water transports vital nutrients and minerals through our bodies and through the soil.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nLife on Earth likely began in the oceans, and all living things—from tiny bacteria to giant blue whales—depend on water. \n*   The human body is made of about **60% water**.\n*   Water acts as a cushion for our organs, regulates our internal temperature, and flushes out waste.\n*   Without water, biological cells cannot carry out the chemical reactions necessary to keep living things alive.\n\n### 5. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. However, almost all of that—about 96.5%—is saltwater found in the oceans, which humans cannot drink or use for crops without expensive desalination processes. \n*   Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**.\n*   Most of that freshwater is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground.\n*   Less than 1% of the Earth's water is easily accessible as fresh surface water (in lakes, rivers, and streams) for human use.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, shaping our weather, carving our landscapes, and sustaining every living creature."
